+Each provider implementation has its own data and authentication boundaries.
+The Claude-specific sections below describe its transcript and status-line path.
+The Codex subsection links its local-state, delegated-request and retention
+details. Claims about a host or another executable require separate evidence.
+
+## Reporting a vulnerability
+
+Open a [security advisory](https://github.com/DarkNightForge/Agent_Efficiency/security/advisories/new)
+rather than a public issue. If advisories are unavailable to you, open an issue
+saying only that you have a security report and asking for a contact — no
+details in the issue text.
+
+Expect an acknowledgement within a week. This is a small project maintained by
+volunteers; there is no bounty and no formal SLA, and pretending otherwise would
+be its own kind of dishonesty.
+
+## What Claude Code reads
+
+| Path | Why | Mode |
+|---|---|---|
+| `~/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl` | token counts and timestamps per turn | read-only |
+| status line JSON, on stdin | `rate_limits`: the vendor's own utilization and reset times | read; the input is passed on unchanged to your own status line |
+| the session transcript's mtime | to date a status line reading | `stat` only, never opened for this |
+| `~/.claude/usage-budget/` | its own derived state | read/write |
+| `~/.claude/settings.json` | to wire and verify hooks and the status line | read; written **only** by `install.sh` |
+
+Transcripts are parsed for three things and no others: the per-turn `usage`
+block, the timestamp, and the **names** of `tool_use` blocks (`Bash`, `Edit`, …)
+so spend can be attributed per tool. Message text is never read, and neither are
+tool *inputs* — the parser takes `b["name"]` from a `tool_use` block and nothing
+else. See the transcript loop in
+[`claude-code/usage_budget.py`](claude-code/usage_budget.py).
+
+Tool names are still information about you: they reveal what kinds of operation
+you run, though not on what. Provider-specific outbound behavior is described below.
+
+## What leaves your machine
+
+Outbound behavior differs by provider:
+
+- **`claude-code/`** makes **no network calls**. The vendor's usage meter
+  arrives from Claude Code itself, as `rate_limits` in the JSON it pipes to
+  your status line; see `record_statusline()` in
+  [`claude-code/usage_budget.py`](claude-code/usage_budget.py).
+
+- **`plugins/` (Codex)** stores local measurement metadata and delegates
+  authenticated `account/rateLimits/read` requests to `codex app-server --stdio`.
+  Hooks and its MCP worker may refresh automatically. The client request does
+  not contain conversation/rollout contents; this does not describe all traffic
+  or credential handling inside Codex. See [the Codex notice](codex/PRIVACY.md).
+- **`hermes/`** never fetches anything itself; it receives Hermes's own
+  `fetch_account_usage` as an injected dependency and reads the result.
+
+These descriptions concern the provider implementations themselves, not every
+network operation of their hosts or injected provider dependencies.
+
+## Credentials
+
+The Claude Code implementation reads no token, no credentials file
+and no environment secret. Your Claude login stays with Claude Code, which
+is where Anthropic's terms keep it
+([legal and compliance](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/legal-and-compliance)).
+
+The Claude hook renders already-recorded state. Codex hooks can launch a bounded
+authenticated usage read; they are not an offline-only path. A timeout bounds
+the client's wait but does not establish all behavior of its external process.
+
+## Codex local state and user control
+
+Codex rollout JSONL is parsed locally for per-call counts, timestamps and scoped
+model/session metadata. Measurement history excludes conversation bodies and
+tool inputs/output. Automatic correlation uses opaque hashes; those references
+remain private metadata, not guaranteed anonymous data. Manually supplied task
+labels may be retained, so use non-sensitive labels.
+
+Plugin-managed writes use an allowlist inside a validated dedicated state
+directory. Original rollouts are not repaired, moved or deleted. These defenses
+do not describe all writes of the installer, Codex or the operating system.
+
+The running MCP worker normally refreshes every 240 seconds and also scans
+rollouts when telemetry is enabled. `telemetry_enabled: false` disables task
+measurement and rollout scanning, retains existing records, and leaves
+allowance refresh and modes enabled. It is not an offline switch. History has
+a configurable record limit and fixed size ceilings; other state has no
+promised age-based expiry. The [Codex notice](codex/PRIVACY.md) gives the details.
+
+The unresolved Windows permission-prompt investigation remains a release gate.
+Functional test passes do not establish its cause or grant credential/passkey
+access. Use supported hook trust and host permission controls.
